WEBVTT

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:08.800
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.730
Yet, certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:22.840
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:28.960
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.770
and some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.450
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd,

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:50.310
the curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.250
The abilities of some plants and animals

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.650
are so remarkable that they seem to be almost supernatural.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.000
In this program, I investigate the shock

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.950
power of a fish that advanced our understanding of electricity and plants

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:12.270
with senses that are surprising modern science. But how do these extraordinary

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:26.230
powers help the organisms that produce them? A freshwater eel is surrounded by

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.000
legends. The first Europeans to explore the new world

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:36.520
heard amazing stories about it and when in the 18th century specimens of this strange fish

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:46.200
reached europe they created a sensation in 1776 captain george baker an american mariner and

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.580
whaler made the long and difficult journey from south america across a raging atlantic ocean

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:58.960
to bring five live electric eels to london these are two of his actual eels

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.920
Captain Baker and his five electric eels, or gymnotus as they were known, set up shop

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.620
in the hay market and offered two shillings and sixpence for a shock, or five shillings

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:18.410
for a spark. Baker's eels had come all the way from the lower reaches of the Amazon and

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:24.130
Orinoco rivers, where he had heard tales from the locals about their astonishing powers.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:30.000
They called these fish trembladores. Humboldt, the

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.260
famous naturalist and explorer had described how he had witnessed horses

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.200
being killed by the repeated shocks from these fish and he himself accidentally

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.350
stepped on one and vividly described the effect with each stroke you feel an

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:50.970
internal vibration that lasts two or three seconds followed by a painful

00:02:50.970 --> 00:03:00.000
numbness all day I felt strong pain in my knees and in all my joints I encountered

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.400
remarkable fish in its natural environment when I filmed at the same

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.470
rivers that Humboldt explored. There was talk of me swimming with the eel but

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.270
thankfully we had some technical difficulties with the diving equipment

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:18.120
that I was supposed to wear and so I stayed safely in the canoe and was able

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.440
to demonstrate another subtler but equally remarkable side to this fish.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:30.000
The eels were constantly producing electric discharges. Some

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.630
Somehow they were generating a small non-stop flowing current.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:42.000
They were also able to sense electricity and were attracted to electrical pulses emitted

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.660
from my underwater detector, suggesting that electricity plays a key role in their lives.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:55.020
But at the time of their discovery, no one knew the full functions of their extraordinary

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.590
abilities.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.000
We now know that the shock

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:07.520
was caused by electricity and i can demonstrate it by touching the animal with an electrode watch

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:15.920
there you see the scope and the lights are flashing up and down extraordinary but this

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.360
is only a small indication of the real power of this fish if i were to try and pick it up

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.110
i could get a jolt of an astonishing 600 volts which is quite enough to kill me

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.150
This 1960s educational film illustrated the shock,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.060
even though the equipment used prevented the volunteers from getting its full power.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.360
They were to join hands and then connected to a live eel.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.800
Believers in electric eels.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.800
Thank you very much.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.000
You can imagine.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:09.950
how startling baker's electric eels were 200 years ago in the 18th century electricity was becoming

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:14.510
one of the most fashionable areas of scientific investigation but it was still very poorly

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:20.130
understood very few advances have been made since its discovery 150 years earlier

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.090
by elizabeth the first personal physician william gilbert gilbert repeated a trick

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:30.000
that had been known about since greek times rubbing a piece of amber with cat fur

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.770
That allowed the amber to attract a small object like a feather.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:35.390
Let's give it a try.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:36.450
Here's a bit of amber.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:41.340
There.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.310
It had always been assumed that this amber effect was caused by magnetism.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.970
But Gilbert showed that it was something different.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:53.360
He named this new force after the Greek word for amber.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:54.340
Electron.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.200
And so, electricity was born.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:00.000
London.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.160
of the time developed a fascination for this magical force. Showmen staged bizarre spectacles

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:13.390
to demonstrate its properties. In one, a young boy attached to a friction generator attracted

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:20.280
small pieces of paper to his hands. In another, a gentleman kissed the lady and was repulsed by

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.860
the charge carried through her whalebone corset.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.000
No one knew what to do with electricity, but a better understanding of its nature was slowly

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.010
emerging. More and more ingenious ways were developed to create what we now call static

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.960
electricity. And soon it became something more than just a quirk of rubbing amber, it

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:52.640
became visible as a spark. The ability to produce this characteristic blue spark, along

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.690
with its invigorating smell, became the signature of this new force. And it prompted scientists

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.000
to make obvious comparisons.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.830
with other natural phenomena.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:09.950
In the American colonies, Benjamin Franklin bravely,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.610
or perhaps foolishly, flew kites into thunderstorms

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.550
and proved that lightning and the electric spark

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.630
were one and the same.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.250
But there's another common property of lightning

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.990
and static electricity, that is the ability to shock.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.050
It wasn't long before a comparison was made

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.000
between the shock from the early generators.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:36.070
and the shock that could be delivered by a fish.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:41.480
The electric eel wasn't the only kind of fish known to give humans a powerful jolt.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:46.480
The ancient Egyptians knew that the electric catfish could also give shocks, and they called

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:50.990
it the Thunderer of the Nile.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.310
And in the nearby Mediterranean lives the torpedo ray.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:08:00.000
Its muscle batteries make it so bulky it can't undulate its body like other rays.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.200
but has to propel itself by waving its tail.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.970
Like the electric eel, it uses its discharge to stun the other fish on which it preys.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:17.750
Sadly, the pressure of celebrity, and having to produce shocks and sparks to order,

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:22.060
exhausted Baker's long-suffering eels, and they didn't last the winter.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:26.670
But two were preserved and expertly dissected by John Hunter,

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.510
the very distinguished Scottish surgeon of the time.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.360
and he found a great number of striped muscular layers

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.310
that proved to be where the electricity was generated.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:40.750
They're now referred to as "hunter's organs".

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:46.430
He found these muscles along the tail and sides of the eels

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:51.960
arranged in stacks. One scientist called Galvani

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:55.470
believed that animals had their own natural electricity

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.000
even without these electric organs, and he tried to prove this

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.680
by connecting wires to frogs' legs and making them twitch.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.990
He called this phenomenon "animal electricity".

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.630
But another scientist called Volta had other ideas.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:21.490
He proved that the frog was merely a conductor for electricity, with a simple experiment.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:28.140
Volta replaced Galfani's frog with discs of cloth soaked in salt water, or acid,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.000
and sandwiched them between two directions.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.380
metals. I can do the same thing with filter paper, copper two-pence pieces, and these

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:53.410
simple galvanized zinc washers. Watch. Company piece, filter, and washer. There. Nearly 0.6

00:09:53.410 --> 00:10:00.000
of a volt. But the amount of electricity generated was tiny. Certainly not as

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.940
enough to make the sparks seen from eels. Unlike Galvani, Volta saw no distinction between

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:12.680
animal electricity and his new electricity from metals, so he now looked to animals to

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:21.150
see how he might amplify his new device. Was it significant that the muscles producing

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:30.000
the electric power in the eels were arranged in stacks? Volta decided to add more stacks

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:34.790
his electric pile we call this way of connecting electric cells together in

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:40.510
series and we now know that it increases the voltage but Volta was about to find

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:44.980
us out for the first time he piled up his tiny cells like the bands of muscle

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:54.320
in an electric fish here I've got ten pairs and just watch nearly six volts

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:00.000
wonderful Volta could now produce heat shocks and even sparks

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:09.280
from electricity in a continuous, never-ending stream. He had made the first battery, partly

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:15.160
inspired by the electric eel. The pieces of the puzzle had come together, and the eel's

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:22.920
example had helped to advance our understanding of electricity. Eels, in fact, contain natural

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:30.000
batteries, stacks of special muscles. It's amazing to think, when the electricity is

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.320
is so much a part of our lives today,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:35.710
that before Volta, the only source of electricity

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:39.180
was lightning, a few static generators,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:45.070
and fish like this incredible electric eel.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:46.950
Understanding how electric eels

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:48.830
managed to find their way around

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:53.950
had revealed a hitherto unknown animal sense.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.870
In part two, we explore the world of plants

00:11:56.870 --> 00:12:00.000
and discover some very strange senses there too.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:07.550
The shocking powers of the electric eel led us to develop our own electrical power.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:15.910
And now we're discovering that plants, too, have intriguing abilities that are still mysterious.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:24.670
We think of plants as passive, still, and silent, but they may have more in common with animals than you might think.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:29.540
New research suggests that they have surprising abilities.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.550
Depends on how you look at them.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.430
I first started seeing plants in a different light

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:43.080
when making a series called The Private Life of Plants.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.230
We used time-lapse photography to reveal the way they move.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:52.970
The bramble spreads aggressively, seemingly unstoppable.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:56.640
Other plants pulse to the rhythms of day and night.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.350
And flower buds explode like fireworks.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:08.040
So with speeded-up film, we've been able to translate their time into ours

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.220
and to realize that they're constantly on the move.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:22.480
A hundred years ago, one plant that moved very quickly indeed

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:25.420
attracted the attention of a great scientific mind.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:30.000
It appeared to behave like an animal and could move faster.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:38.630
enough to catch its own food. Charles Darwin was fascinated by the Venus flytrap. He called

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:44.110
it one of the most wonderful plants in the world. He recognized that it could move in

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:50.200
a very different way to that of plant growth. This movement was not only fast, but also

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:55.130
repeatable. Darwin experimented and found that the traps were not triggered by raindrops,

00:13:55.130 --> 00:14:00.000
but only by a very particular stimulation of the leaf hairs, such as

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:07.430
an insect might make. But what intrigued him most was the speed of the reaction. He sent

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:12.290
one of these fly traps to a friend, Dr. Burden Sanderson, who was performing groundbreaking

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:18.770
work on muscles and electricity. His tests confirmed that the tiny electrical discharge

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:24.150
caused by an animal muscle cell contracting was almost identical to those signals obtained

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:30.000
by attaching electrodes to the fly trap when it was shutting.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.680
plants have no muscles, electrical stimulation enables them to move in a

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:41.320
similar way to animals. Electrical signals cause cells to change the

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:47.450
pressure of sap in their leaves, so creating movement. As a result, some

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:51.840
plants, like animals, can actively catch their prey.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:15:00.000
Recently, it's been discovered that other plants use electricity too, but for

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.290
a very different purpose. Plants are rooted to the ground and have a small negative charge.

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:14.330
The higher up the plant you go, the greater the electric charge. This creates an electric field

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:20.730
around the flower. We can't see it, but these electrodes are picking up the energy of this tiny

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:27.760
field and converting it into the sound that we can hear. Bees, on the other hand, have a positive

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.000
charge. Friction whilst flying

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:36.000
causes them to lose electrons leaving them electrically charged as a bee

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.750
approaches a flower the charge fields around the flower and the bee interact

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:48.460
and the sound changes there and when it lands the positive and negative fields

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:53.820
immediately cancel each other out as this happens there are two very surprising

00:15:53.820 --> 00:16:00.000
consequences firstly the plants negatively charged pollen actually jumps across

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.440
onto the positively charged bee.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.800
Secondly, the plant has a changed electrical field,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.200
and when another bee comes along,

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:13.700
it detects this altered electrical signature and avoids the flower.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:19.730
The plant is, in effect, telling the bee that it has no nectar and to come back later.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.790
When the flower has refilled its stores of nectar,

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:30.000
it creates a new electric charge which attracts another passing bee.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:38.850
This simple on/off signal benefits both the bee and the flower, but it does have its limitations.

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:45.070
The electrical field is tiny, so insects can only detect it at close quarters.

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:50.820
But flowers can also draw attention to themselves over much greater distances,

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:55.460
and they do this by floating messages in the air.

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:59.480
The perfume of a flower is not just a pleasant smell.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.450
also the primary way in which plants communicate with insects. A rose can

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:10.270
contain over 400 chemical compounds and a bee can recognize a particular

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:16.080
combination from over a mile away. The very latest research has discovered that

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:22.860
90% of the chemicals made by plants are also produced by insects and that is no

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:30.000
coincidence. Most flowers produce scent to persuade insects

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:39.340
to visit them. But others use it in a more sophisticated way. For protection, cabbages

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:46.410
communicate with each other using smell. When the leaves of one plant are being attacked

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:53.220
by caterpillars, it releases a scent, which warns its neighbors. They then produce chemicals

00:17:53.220 --> 00:18:00.000
in their leaves that caterpillars don't like, and so they avoid being eaten. And scent

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.600
also serves to call in the cavalry.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:07.590
Leaves that are under attack give off a chemical alarm signal

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:12.890
that attracts wasps, which obligingly pick off the caterpillar attackers.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.660
So, vegetables, fruits, leaves, and flowers

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.360
are constantly communicating with each other

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.330
using touch, vision, and smell.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.890
They seem to exploit all the senses, apart.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.920
that is, from hearing.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.100
But there are old stories that one particular plant

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.780
is able to produce a very strange sound.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:41.580
Hundreds of years ago,

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.320
a plant with a root that was thought to resemble a human body

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.850
was said to emit a sound that could kill.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:51.860
The root was known to have strong anesthetic

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.520
and hallucinogenic properties,

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:55.310
and in the first century AD,

00:18:55.830 --> 00:19:00.000
it was called a mandroga, or mandrake, as it's now known.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:07.300
It was associated with magic and the supernatural, and was thought to derive power from a demon

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:12.070
that emitted a dreadful and fatal shriek if the plant was uprooted.

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:17.410
Fortunately, there were creative ways of avoiding death from the killer sound.

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:23.450
One account advised plugging one's ears and then tying a starving dog to the mandrake

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:30.000
plant, and then as the dog lunged for food, the plant would be uprooted.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.020
The dog would tragically die from the mandrake's shriek, but the man would survive.

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:44.830
This particular story may have arisen because drinks made with the mandrake root can produce hallucinations,

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:56.500
but we're just beginning to realize that the sensory abilities of a root could be as sophisticated as the rest of the plant.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:10.400
Latest research suggests that roots are communicating underground, and we now have the technology

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:14.790
to eavesdrop on the roots world.

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:23.350
Believe it or not, the roots of these corn seedlings can make and sense sound. The noise

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:30.000
is very quiet, but we can hear it with this equipment. If I place a corn seedling in front

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:41.610
of a laser beam like this now the sound vibration can be detected and we can hear it through a

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:52.700
speaker there that strange crackling is the sound of corn roots growing it can be seen as pulses

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:58.540
on the screen it's been shown too that the corn roots respond to the sound when it's played back

00:20:58.540 --> 00:20:59.090
to them

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:07.120
Time-lapse footage shot over just a few hours clearly shows the roots growing towards the tiny speakers that emit the sound.

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:12.160
There's much speculation about the purpose of this curious phenomenon.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:19.060
Perhaps it helps roots avoid growing into hard objects or being too close to competing plants.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:24.620
It could act like simple echolocation. We just don't know.

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:29.810
But it's the first clear evidence that plants have a rudimentary form of hearing.

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:34.040
and might even be communicating underground using sound.

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:38.490
Sensitive equipment is creating a new window into the plant world

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:40.600
and it seems that, like animals,

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.640
they have a sophisticated sense of their environment

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:46.760
and possess abilities that not so long ago

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:49.810
we would have thought of as supernatural.
